+/// Where dynamic rate control holds the ring, as a fraction of its capacity.
+///
+/// Upstream holds it at half, which makes the ring's occupancy and its headroom
+/// the same number. That is fine where the sink drains steadily. The MI_AO sink
+/// does not: it writes only while the codec's queue is under target and sleeps
+/// otherwise, so the ring fills between bursts -- measured at 2573 frames
+/// against a 3200 capacity -- and `push_blocking` then stalls the emulator
+/// waiting for room. The core sat at 51 Hz, 10% short on audio, with the ring
+/// reporting no starvation at all because the deficit had moved to the codec.
+///
+/// A quarter keeps the occupancy, and so the latency, where it was while giving
+/// the ring three times the headroom before it can block the core.
 pub fn drc_target(capacity_frames: usize) -> usize {
     capacity_frames / 2
 }
